  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Protagonists of War shows early evidence of reach and public visibility: it has been downloaded 2,470 times through OAPEN and appears in public reference sources, including Wikipedia and Crossref-linked mentions. The book was published by Leuven University Press in 2021 and is supported by NWO Free competition funding. Its citation record in OpenAlex is still at zero, so the current picture is one of readership and discovery rather than scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Raymond Fagel\u2019s Protagonists of War examines how Spanish military commanders from the first decade of the Revolt in the Low Countries were represented across a wide range of war narratives, from chronicles and poetry to theatre, engravings, songs, and news pamphlets. That topic aligns with the book\u2019s strongest conceptual signals around narrative, reading, literature, poetry, and history, and it is reflected in the record as a work designed to speak to both historical scholarship and broader interpretive questions about storytelling in wartime.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal so far is use. OAPEN metrics show 2,470 downloads, with activity spread across the year rather than confined to a single burst. This suggests that the book is being accessed as an ongoing resource, likely through open or hybrid availability rather than only through purchase or library lending. OpenAIRE also records the book as hybrid open access, with open instances available, which helps explain the visible readership.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book also has public visibility beyond formal scholarship. The record shows mentions in Wikipedia and Crossref-linked sources, which places it in public knowledge infrastructure and indicates that it has been noticed in general reference and curated web contexts. There is evidence of public discourse around the work in historical-archive data, but the available record does not support a finer-grained interpretation of that attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record remains quiet, with zero citations in OpenAlex. That does not preclude future influence; it simply means that the current evidence base is centered on access, discoverability, and public reference rather than citation-based reuse. The book\u2019s funded publication context and peer-review signal point to a conventional scholarly route to publication, while the open-access availability appears to be supporting its present-day reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, Protagonists of War is best described as a book with demonstrable readership and public visibility, but limited citation-based uptake so far. Its impact profile is strongest in dissemination and accessibility, and the record suggests a work that is available, findable, and being consulted, even as formal scholarly reuse has yet to accumulate.</p>",
